{
  "concept": "Because the unit-circle sector of angle x is squeezed between an inner triangle of area sin x / 2 and an outer triangle of area tan x / 2, the inequality sin x ≤ x ≤ tan x forces sin x / x → 1 as x → 0.",
  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "Left panel: x- and y-axes with arrowheads, centered at origin",
    "Left panel: blue unit circle of radius 1",
    "Left panel: red radius from origin to the point (cos x, sin x) on the circle, extended to (1, tan x)",
    "Left panel: dark red horizontal segment from origin to (1, 0) labeled with a brace and the label '1'",
    "Left panel: vertical dashed red tangent segment at x = 1 from (1, 0) up to (1, tan x)",
    "Left panel: dashed red chord from (cos x, sin x) to (1, 0)",
    "Left panel: three red dots at (1, 0), (cos x, sin x), (1, tan x)",
    "Left panel: labels '(1, 0)', '(cos x, sin x)' with leader arrow, '(1, tan x)'",
    "Left panel: small arc with angle label 'x' at the origin",
    "Right panel A: large right triangle, red outline, pale pink fill, base labeled '1', vertical leg labeled 'tan x', dashed blue arc of the unit circle cutting across it, angle 'x' at the left vertex",
    "Right panel B: circular sector, red straight edges, pale pink fill, solid blue arc, base labeled '1', angle 'x' at vertex",
    "Right panel C: small right triangle with vertices at origin, (1,0) and the circle point; red outline, pale pink fill, base labeled '1', dashed vertical red segment of height sin x with brace label 'sin x', dashed blue arc bulging outside the hypotenuse, angle 'x' at vertex",
    "Caption row: 'Area of triangle  ≥  Area of sector  ≥  Area of triangle'",
    "Formula row: 'tan x / 2  ≥  x / 2  ≥  sin x / 2'",
    "Numeric readout of current x (radians), sin x, tan x, and the three area values",
    "Derived-inequality annotation: 'cos x ≤ sin x / x ≤ 1 / cos x'"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "angleX",
      "type": "slider",
      "teaches": "That the sector's area is trapped between the two triangle areas for every angle, and that the three areas converge onto each other as x shrinks toward 0",
      "label": "Angle x (radians)",
      "range": [0.05, 1.5, 0.01],
      "default": 0.8
    },
    {
      "id": "overlayMode",
      "type": "toggle",
      "teaches": "That the three shapes are nested regions of the same unit circle, not three unrelated pictures — the inner triangle sits inside the sector, which sits inside the outer triangle",
      "label": "View",
      "options": ["Side-by-side", "Overlaid"],
      "default": "Side-by-side"
    },
    {
      "id": "highlightRegion",
      "type": "click",
      "teaches": "Which region each caption refers to and how its area formula (tan x / 2, x / 2, sin x / 2) is built from the unit-circle geometry",
      "teachesNote": "click a panel caption or region to isolate it and show its formula"
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "The unit circle setup",
      "narration": "A ray at angle x from the positive x-axis meets the unit circle at (cos x, sin x) and the vertical tangent line at (1, tan x). Everything in the argument is built from these three points and the base of length 1.",
      "state": { "angleX": 0.8, "overlayMode": "Side-by-side" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Three regions, three areas",
      "narration": "The large triangle has area tan x / 2, the sector has area x / 2 (since the unit circle's sector area is x/2 for radius 1), and the small triangle has area sin x / 2. Click each caption to isolate a region and see its formula.",
      "state": { "angleX": 0.8, "overlayMode": "Side-by-side" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Nesting revealed",
      "narration": "Overlay the three shapes on one unit-circle panel: the small triangle sits inside the sector, which sits inside the big triangle. Containment of regions is exactly why sin x / 2 ≤ x / 2 ≤ tan x / 2.",
      "state": { "angleX": 0.8, "overlayMode": "Overlaid" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Widen the angle",
      "narration": "Push x toward π/2 and the outer triangle blows up while the inner triangle barely grows — the bound is loose for large angles, so sin x / x is far from 1 here.",
      "state": { "angleX": 1.4, "overlayMode": "Overlaid" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Squeeze toward zero",
      "narration": "Shrink x toward 0 and the three shapes become nearly indistinguishable: tan x / 2, x / 2 and sin x / 2 collapse together. Dividing the inequality by sin x / 2 gives cos x ≤ sin x / x ≤ 1 / cos x, and both bounds tend to 1.",
      "state": { "angleX": 0.1, "overlayMode": "Overlaid" }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "The sector is geometrically sandwiched between two triangles, so its area x/2 is sandwiched between sin x / 2 and tan x / 2 — and as x → 0 the sandwich closes, forcing sin x / x to 1.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Two-part layout: a left square panel with the unit circle, and a right band containing three shapes in a row (large triangle, sector, small triangle) with two centered caption rows beneath them ('Area of triangle ≥ Area of sector ≥ Area of triangle' and 'tan x / 2 ≥ x / 2 ≥ sin x / 2'). Color code: circle and arcs in medium blue (dashed blue where the arc is not a boundary of the shape), all straight construction lines and shape outlines in salmon red, shape interiors in pale pink wash, the base segment from origin to (1,0) in dark red, point markers as solid red dots, axes and all text in black. Left panel labels: '(1, tan x)' upper right, '(cos x, sin x)' with a short leader arrow to the circle point, '(1, 0)' at the x-axis point, angle 'x' inside a small arc at the origin, and a curly brace under the base labeled '1'. Right panel labels: 'tan x' on the vertical leg of the large triangle, 'sin x' with a curly brace at the right of the small triangle, '1' under each base, 'x' at each left vertex. In the overlaid view, draw one unit circle with all three regions superimposed using differing fill opacities and a shared legend keyed to tan x / 2, x / 2, sin x / 2, and show a live readout of the three numeric areas plus the derived inequality cos x ≤ sin x / x ≤ 1 / cos x. Clicking a caption or region should dim the others and enlarge its formula."
}

